<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Polaris with the real CAD hull geometry, satisfying the model contract in
  src/boat_simulator_gazebo/README.md.

  This model and models/polaris_basic are deliberately identical everywhere except
  <visual> geometry: same link and joint names, same mass and inertia, same joint
  limits and gains, same LiftDrag and Hydrodynamics parameters. polaris_basic draws
  itself with boxes; this one draws itself with the CAD meshes. Keeping the physics
  byte-for-byte identical means switching boat_model_sdf between them changes only
  what you see, never how the boat behaves, and there is one set of numbers to
  maintain rather than two.

  Every physical quantity therefore comes from BOAT_PROPERTIES in
  boat_simulator/common/constants.py, NOT from the CAD export. The SolidWorks export
  reports 244.28 kg and I_xx = 11.1 kg.m^2; BOAT_PROPERTIES says 276 kg and
  I_xx = 175.86. The CAD figures cover the bare structure rather than the rigged boat,
  and the roll inertia in particular is low enough to make the keel's LiftDrag moment
  diverge and abort gz-sim within ~20 s.

  Frames: x forward (bow), y port, z up, with the model origin ON THE CG, matching the
  convention the *_CE_REL_TO_CG constants are expressed against.

  The meshes in meshes/ are NOT the raw CAD export. tools/prepare_polaris_meshes.py
  rotates them out of the exporter's z-down frame (raw, the wingsail hangs below the
  hull and the rudder points at the sky), moves the hull onto the CG, and decimates
  base_hull from 1.35 M triangles to ~36 k. See that script for the details; the raw
  exports live in meshes/raw/ and are gitignored.
-->
<sdf version="1.9">
  <model name="polaris">
    <!-- Hull: carries the boat's full mass and inertia, and all of the displaced volume.
         The keel is rigidly fixed, so it is part of this link rather than its own. -->
    <link name="hull">
      <inertial>
        <!-- Ballast offset: the CG sits 0.35 m BELOW the link origin, standing in for the
             lead a real keelboat carries low in its keel. Without it this boat capsizes
             and floats inverted, because form stability alone leaves a hull with two
             stable equilibria and no preference for which way is up. Same value and same
             reasoning as models/polaris_basic -- see the long comment there. -->
        <pose>0 0 -0.35 0 0 0</pose>
        <mass>276.0</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>175.86</ixx>
          <ixy>0</ixy>
          <ixz>0</ixz>
          <!-- I_yy is not tracked by the 4-DOF kinematic model (no pitch DOF). Estimated
               from the hull's slenderness; pitch is lightly excited in practice. -->
          <iyy>160.0</iyy>
          <iyz>0</iyz>
          <izz>119.04</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>

      <!-- Buoyancy proxy, NOT the hull mesh. Two separate reasons it has to be a box:

           1. base_hull.STL is a thin-walled shell, about 10 mm thick -- a vertical ray
              through it crosses the surface in pairs 9-11 mm apart. The volume it
              encloses is the volume of the hull *skin* (0.177 m^3), not the volume the
              hull *displaces*. Handing it to Hydrodynamics would compute buoyancy from
              the amount of fibreglass, which is meaningless and, at 0.177 m^3 < the
              0.269 m^3 needed, would not even float the boat.
           2. Hydrodynamics re-integrates pressure over this geometry every physics step.
              Against the full-resolution mesh that alone drops the simulation to roughly
              0.01 s of sim time per 7 s of wall clock.

           The dimensions are polaris_basic's, NOT the real hull's, and that is
           deliberate. Sized instead to the real hull's measured hydrostatics (draft
           0.33 m, waterplane 2.02 m^2, footprint 3.0 x 0.673), the boat is far too
           tender: the real hull's waterplane second moment is only 0.061 m^4 against
           this box's 0.291 m^4, giving a metacentric height of 0.10-0.21 m rather than
           1.04 m. That is the same order as the couple formed by the sail's side force
           2 m above the CG and the keel's 1.13 m below it, so the boat heels heavily
           under ambient wind alone. The ballast offset above is what keeps it upright,
           but there is no reason to make its job harder than it needs to be.

           Only the box's vertical placement differs from polaris_basic's: it sits high
           enough on the origin that the waterline lands at z = -0.006, which is where
           the real hull mesh floats at this displacement. The boat is then as stable as
           the verified model while the visible hull still sits correctly in the water.

               draft = 0.269 / (3.6 x 0.99)        = 0.0754 m
               GM    = KB + BM - KG
                     = -0.044 + 1.082 - (-0.35)    = 1.39 m   (polaris_basic: 1.19 m)

           Equilibrium puts the origin 0.006 m above the waterline, so this model spawns
           at z ~ 0, whereas polaris_basic spawns at 0.2 (see boat_spawn_height in
           gazebo_launch.py). -->
      <collision name="hull_collision">
        <pose>-0.135 0 0.1936 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>3.6 0.99 0.55</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
      </collision>

      <!-- The keel is visual-only: it is a thin foil whose displacement is negligible
           beside the hull's, and the box above is already sized to float the whole boat.
           Its hydrodynamic side force comes from the LiftDrag plugin further down. -->
      <visual name="hull_visual">
        <geometry>
          <mesh>
            <uri>meshes/base_hull.STL</uri>
          </mesh>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.85 0.85 0.88 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.9 0.9 0.95 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>

      <!-- Wind must reach the sail link via the world's WindEffects system. -->
      <enable_wind>false</enable_wind>
    </link>

    <!-- Wing sail: rotates about the mast, which the CAD places 2.03 m above the CG and
         0.27 m aft of it. The kinematic model drives the wing indirectly through the trim
         tab, so the mast joint is free-spinning-with-damping and the trim tab is the
         actuated surface, matching the real boat. -->
    <link name="wing_sail">
      <pose>-0.2693 0.11054 2.02723 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>12.0</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>6.0</ixx>
          <ixy>0</ixy>
          <ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>6.0</iyy>
          <iyz>0</iyz>
          <izz>0.6</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <collision name="wing_sail_collision">
        <pose>-0.5496 -0.11 -0.282 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>1.532 0.08 3.55</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
      </collision>
      <visual name="wing_sail_visual">
        <geometry>
          <mesh>
            <uri>meshes/link_wingsail.STL</uri>
          </mesh>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.95 0.95 0.95 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>1 1 1 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>

      <!-- The wing's planform, as a plate rather than geometry, because THE CAD EXPORT
           DOES NOT CONTAIN THE SAIL SKIN. link_wingsail.STL is the structural frame only:
           a mast tube spanning z -2.06..1.49 and two spars at z 0.10 and 0.78, 1.99 m^2 of
           surface in total. A 2.01 m^2 wing would need about 4 m^2 of skin on its own, and
           the mesh has none of it -- rendered as exported, the boat carries a bare mast and
           no sail. -->
      <visual name="wing_sail_planform_visual">
        <pose>0.2 -0.11 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <mesh>
            <uri>meshes/wingsail_planform.STL</uri>
          </mesh>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.95 0.95 0.95 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>1 1 1 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <enable_wind>true</enable_wind>
    </link>

    <!-- Trim tab: the actuated surface, 1.36 m aft of the mast axis in the CAD (the
         kinematic model's WINGSAIL_TO_TRIM_TAB_BOOM_LENGTH is 1.43 m). -->
    <link name="trim_tab">
      <pose>-1.633 -0.03204 2.45329 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>1.2</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.4</ixx>
          <ixy>0</ixy>
          <ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>0.4</iyy>
          <iyz>0</iyz>
          <izz>0.05</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <collision name="trim_tab_collision">
        <pose>-0.0078 0.0257 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.31 0.075 0.63</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
      </collision>
      <visual name="trim_tab_visual">
        <geometry>
          <mesh>
            <uri>meshes/link_trim_tab.STL</uri>
          </mesh>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.9 0.6 0.1 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.9 0.6 0.1 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <enable_wind>true</enable_wind>
    </link>

    <!-- Rudder: the CAD stock is 1.36 m aft of the CG, matching RUDDER_CE_REL_TO_CG's
         -1.35 m almost exactly. -->
    <link name="rudder">
      <pose>-1.36179 0.00955 -0.57477 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>3.0</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.3</ixx>
          <ixy>0</ixy>
          <ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>0.3</iyy>
          <iyz>0</iyz>
          <izz>0.05</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <collision name="rudder_collision">
        <pose>0.0707 -0.0095 0.2016 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.492 0.037 0.931</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
      </collision>
      <visual name="rudder_visual">
        <geometry>
          <mesh>
            <uri>meshes/link_rudder.STL</uri>
          </mesh>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.2 0.2 0.25 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.2 0.2 0.25 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>

    <!-- JOINTS -->

    <!-- Mast: free to weathervane about +Z. Not in the contract's actuated-joint list; the
         trim tab is what steers it. Damping stands in for mast bearing friction and keeps
         the wing from oscillating unboundedly. -->
    <joint name="mast_joint" type="revolute">
      <parent>hull</parent>
      <child>wing_sail</child>
      <axis>
        <xyz>0 0 1</xyz>
        <limit>
          <lower>-3.14159</lower>
          <upper>3.14159</upper>
        </limit>
        <dynamics>
          <damping>2.0</damping>
        </dynamics>
      </axis>
    </joint>

    <!-- Contract joint: trim_tab_joint, revolute about +Z, CCW positive, +/-40 deg. -->
    <joint name="trim_tab_joint" type="revolute">
      <parent>wing_sail</parent>
      <child>trim_tab</child>
      <axis>
        <xyz>0 0 1</xyz>
        <limit>
          <lower>-0.698</lower>
          <upper>0.698</upper>
        </limit>
        <dynamics>
          <damping>0.1</damping>
        </dynamics>
      </axis>
    </joint>

    <!-- Contract joint: rudder_joint, revolute about +Z, CCW positive, +/-30 deg.
         The CAD rakes the stock about 21 deg off vertical; the contract specifies a
         +Z axis, and a raked axis would make the commanded angle and the achieved
         steering angle differ by cos(rake), so the axis is held vertical here. -->
    <joint name="rudder_joint" type="revolute">
      <parent>hull</parent>
      <child>rudder</child>
      <axis>
        <xyz>0 0 1</xyz>
        <limit>
          <lower>-0.524</lower>
          <upper>0.524</upper>
        </limit>
        <dynamics>
          <damping>0.5</damping>
        </dynamics>
      </axis>
    </joint>

    <!-- PLUGINS -->

    <!-- Pose/velocity feedback consumed by gazebo_physics_bridge_node, bridged to
         /gazebo/odometry. Publishes on /model/polaris/odometry by default. -->
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-odometry-publisher-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::OdometryPublisher">
      <dimensions>3</dimensions>
      <odom_publish_frequency>10</odom_publish_frequency>
      <odom_frame>world</odom_frame>
      <robot_base_frame>polaris</robot_base_frame>
    </plugin>

    <!-- Actuated joints. Topics must match BRIDGED_TOPICS in launch/gazebo_launch.py. -->
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-joint-position-controller-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::JointPositionController">
      <joint_name>rudder_joint</joint_name>
      <topic>/model/polaris/joint/rudder_joint/cmd_pos</topic>
      <p_gain>8.0</p_gain>
      <i_gain>0.0</i_gain>
      <d_gain>1.0</d_gain>
    </plugin>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-joint-position-controller-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::JointPositionController">
      <joint_name>trim_tab_joint</joint_name>
      <topic>/model/polaris/joint/trim_tab_joint/cmd_pos</topic>
      <p_gain>3.0</p_gain>
      <i_gain>0.0</i_gain>
      <d_gain>0.3</d_gain>
    </plugin>

    <!-- Aerodynamics. cla/cda and the stall point approximate the BOAT_PROPERTIES
         coefficient tables: the sail's CL peaks near 1.20 at ~25 deg, so cla ~ 2.75/rad
         with stall at 0.44 rad. <upward> is the lift axis; <forward> is the chord line. -->
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-lift-drag-system" name="gz::sim::systems::LiftDrag">
      <link_name>wing_sail</link_name>
      <air_density>1.225</air_density>
      <area>2.01</area>
      <a0>0.0</a0>
      <cla>2.75</cla>
      <cda>0.05</cda>
      <alpha_stall>0.44</alpha_stall>
      <cla_stall>-2.7</cla_stall>
      <cda_stall>1.5</cda_stall>
      <cp>0 0 0</cp>
      <forward>1 0 0</forward>
      <upward>0 1 0</upward>
    </plugin>

    <plugin filename="gz-sim-lift-drag-system" name="gz::sim::systems::LiftDrag">
      <link_name>trim_tab</link_name>
      <air_density>1.225</air_density>
      <area>0.198</area>
      <a0>0.0</a0>
      <cla>2.75</cla>
      <cda>0.05</cda>
      <alpha_stall>0.44</alpha_stall>
      <cla_stall>-2.7</cla_stall>
      <cda_stall>1.5</cda_stall>
      <cp>0 0 0</cp>
      <forward>1 0 0</forward>
      <upward>0 1 0</upward>
    </plugin>

    <!-- Hydrodynamics: same LiftDrag system, seawater density. The rudder's CL peaks near
         0.92 at ~20 deg -> cla ~ 2.6/rad, stall at 0.35 rad. -->
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-lift-drag-system" name="gz::sim::systems::LiftDrag">
      <link_name>rudder</link_name>
      <air_density>1027</air_density>
      <area>0.117</area>
      <a0>0.0</a0>
      <cla>2.6</cla>
      <cda>0.02</cda>
      <alpha_stall>0.35</alpha_stall>
      <cla_stall>-2.5</cla_stall>
      <cda_stall>1.2</cda_stall>
      <cp>0 0 0</cp>
      <forward>1 0 0</forward>
      <upward>0 1 0</upward>
    </plugin>

    <!-- Keel: attached to the hull link, with <cp> at KEEL_CE_REL_TO_CG so the side force
         and its righting/leeway moment are applied at the correct point.

         This offset is only safe because the link it acts on carries BOAT_PROPERTIES'
         I_xx of 175.86 kg.m^2. With the CAD export's I_xx of 11.1 the same block spins
         the hull fast enough that <forward>/<upward> rotate into a new orientation every
         step, and gz-sim aborts on an ODE broad-phase assertion within ~20 s. If this
         model's inertia is ever taken back from CAD, this offset has to be revisited. -->
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-lift-drag-system" name="gz::sim::systems::LiftDrag">
      <link_name>hull</link_name>
      <air_density>1027</air_density>
      <area>0.51</area>
      <a0>0.0</a0>
      <cla>2.75</cla>
      <cda>0.05</cda>
      <alpha_stall>0.44</alpha_stall>
      <cla_stall>-2.7</cla_stall>
      <cda_stall>1.5</cda_stall>
      <cp>0.08 0 -1.13</cp>
      <forward>1 0 0</forward>
      <upward>0 1 0</upward>
    </plugin>

    <!-- Flotation and hydrodynamic damping, from asv_wave_sim.

         This replaces the stock gz-sim Buoyancy system entirely. Rather than applying a
         single buoyant force at a fixed point, it meshes the hull's collision geometry and
         integrates pressure over whichever part is currently below the wave surface. The
         centre of buoyancy therefore moves outboard as the boat heels, producing a real
         waterplane righting moment. That is what lets the hull's inertial pose stay at the
         CG instead of carrying an artificial ballast offset.

         <enable> takes fully-qualified model::link names. Only the hull is listed: it holds
         all of the displaced volume, while the rig sits above the waterline and the
         rudder's small volume is not worth the per-step mesh integration.

         The <wave> block MUST match the WavesModel plugin in worlds/sailing_world.sdf.
         Both evaluate the wave field independently from these parameters, so a mismatch
         floats the boat against a different surface than the one being rendered. -->
    <plugin filename="gz-waves1-hydrodynamics-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Hydrodynamics">
      <enable>polaris::hull</enable>

      <wave>
        <algorithm>fft</algorithm>
        <tile_size>256</tile_size>
        <cell_count>256</cell_count>
        <wind_speed>1.96</wind_speed>
        <wind_angle_deg>45</wind_angle_deg>
        <steepness>2</steepness>
      </wave>

      <hydrodynamics>
        <damping_on>1</damping_on>
        <viscous_drag_on>1</viscous_drag_on>
        <pressure_drag_on>1</pressure_drag_on>

        <!-- Linear and angular damping. The kinematic backend's linear damping matrix
             D = diag(45, 180, 115, 140) in constants.py is in N.s/m, whereas these are
             dimensionless coefficients scaled by the submerged area, so they cannot be
             copied across directly. Left at the asv_wave_sim reference values; tune
             against the kinematic backend's response if the two need to agree. -->
        <cDampL1>1.0E-6</cDampL1>
        <cDampL2>1.0E-6</cDampL2>
        <cDampR1>1.0E-6</cDampR1>
        <cDampR2>1.0E-6</cDampR2>

        <!-- Pressure drag. Stands in for the hull_drag_factor = 0.5 quadratic hull drag
             in constants.py, which this plugin resolves per-face instead. -->
        <cPDrag1>1.0E+2</cPDrag1>
        <cPDrag2>1.0E+2</cPDrag2>
        <fPDrag>0.4</fPDrag>
        <cSDrag1>1.0E+2</cSDrag1>
        <cSDrag2>1.0E+2</cSDrag2>
        <fSDrag>0.4</fSDrag>
        <vRDrag>1.0</vRDrag>
      </hydrodynamics>
    </plugin>
  </model>
</sdf>
